### Hillclimb

**You own the metric and the experiment's integrity. Supervise, review. Delegate the attempts.** For sustained iterative improvement of one measurable thing against a target. A one-off fix is [Bug fix](bug-fix.md) or [Perf issue](perf-issue.md). This is the loop.

Core discipline: one change, one measurement, keep or revert. Never stack untested changes. Never claim a win from code inspection (the [prove it works](../../prove-it-works/SKILL.md) principle skill).

1. Ground workload and architecture before choosing the metric. Run the [how](../../how/SKILL.md) skill over the target, name the realistic workload dimensions that can move the result (data size, history, state, concurrency), select a case reproducing the user's complaint. No case reproduces it? Fix the repro, don't hillclimb. Then fix one metric, the direction that counts as better, and a checkable stop predicate pairing a target with a floor on attempts so a lucky early win can't end the run (example "at least 50% better than baseline and at least 10 iterations"). Use the user's numbers when given, otherwise agree them.
2. Build the measurement harness, prove its sensitivity, freeze it (the [build the lever](../../build-the-lever/SKILL.md) principle skill). Run contrasting realistic workloads, confirm the target case reproduces the symptom while easier cases separate as expected. Harness can't distinguish them? Revise workload or metric. Frozen: one repeatable command emits the metric, sampled enough to clear the noise (median of N, not one run). Record baseline metric and a green run of the regression gate (tests that must keep passing) before any change.
3. Open the decision log via the [show me your work](../../show-me-your-work/SKILL.md) skill. `decision.tsv`, one row per attempt: id, hypothesis, change, before, after, delta, tests, verdict (kept or reverted), note. Read it before each attempt. Keep it out of the tree (gitignored).
4. Ground each hypothesis in the architecture model from step 1, so it names a specific mechanism ("defer X off the boot path because it blocks first paint"), not "try memoizing something".
5. Loop, one hypothesis per iteration:
   - Hand the change to a subagent with a tight scope. Supervise and review the diff, don't type it (the [context window guard](../../context-window-guard/SKILL.md) principle skill). Several independent hypotheses live? Fan them to parallel subagents, each in its own worktree (the [separate before serializing shared state](../../separate-before-serializing-shared-state/SKILL.md) principle skill).
   - Measure before and after with the frozen harness, run the regression gate.
   - Accept only when the metric moves past noise and the gate stays green. Otherwise revert in full. A tweak that "might help" is not kept.
   - One commit per accepted fix, staging only changed files (`git add <files>`, never `-A`). Log the row either way, kept or reverted.
   Each iteration ends in a check before the next begins (the [sequence verifiable units](../../sequence-verifiable-units/SKILL.md) principle skill). Run unattended? Borrow only the wake mechanism from the [Autonomous run](autonomous-run.md) playbook, not its stop rule.
6. Push past the first plateau. Stall, several rejects in a row? Pivot category, combine near-misses, re-read the source, or try something more radical before concluding the hill is climbed. Correctness and simplicity outrank the number. Revert a win that breaks behavior; keep a simplification that holds the number (the [laziness protocol](../../laziness-protocol/SKILL.md) principle skill).
7. Stop when the predicate is met, or when remaining ideas are marginal and not worth their cost. Don't relax the predicate. Don't quit while cheap untried hypotheses remain. Stuck? Surface it, don't spin.
8. Run [Opening a PR](opening-a-pr.md) with accepted commits stacked in landing order.

**Reply:** metric and target, baseline to final with percent delta, iterations run (kept vs reverted), each accepted fix one line, `decision.tsv` path, best idea you'd try next.
